    <div class="chapter reveal"><span class="bignum">01</span><div class="num">Chapter One</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="identity">Why it's identity, not discipline</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">Most "discipline problems" are identity problems in disguise. You don't struggle to act like someone you already believe you are — you struggle to act like someone you're still trying to convince yourself you could be.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Notice how effortless the habits are that match your self-image. You don't white-knuckle brushing your teeth; it's just what someone like you does. <strong>Behaviour that matches identity needs no discipline — it needs no push at all.</strong> The exhausting fight only happens when the action and the self-image disagree. Fix the self-image and the fight ends.</p>
    <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>Stop asking "how do I get myself to do this?" and start asking "who is the person for whom this is normal?" The first question is a lifetime of forcing. The second is a decision you can make today — and then simply act from.</p></div>

