    <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">02</span><div class="num">Chapter Two</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="shift">The Creator to CEO shift</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">The Creator to CEO shift is the move from seeing yourself as someone who makes things to someone who runs a business. It's the mindset change underneath every practical skill — because you build differently once you own the outcome, not just the output.</p>
    <p class="reveal">A creator makes and hopes. A CEO decides and directs. The difference isn't the tasks — it's the posture. <strong>A creator asks "what should I make?" A CEO asks "what does this business need me to decide?"</strong> That shift changes what you build, what you ignore, and how you spend the one resource you can't make more of.</p>

    <div class="photo-quote reveal">
      <div class="photo dark" style="position:absolute;inset:0;background-image:url(/images/library/portrait-woman-golden-hour.jpg);background-size:cover;background-position:center"></div>
      <div class="veil"></div>
      <div class="inner"><p>A creator makes things and hopes. A CEO decides what gets made — and owns what happens next.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="box mistake reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✕</span> Common Mistake</span><p>Staying a creator forever because making is comfortable and deciding is scary. Endless creating can be a sophisticated way to avoid ownership — always one more thing to build, never the hard call to make. Growth lives in the deciding.</p></div>

    <div class="chapter reveal"><span class="bignum">03</span><div class="num">Chapter Three</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="ready">The trap of waiting to feel ready</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">You will never feel ready before you start — readiness is something you build by starting, not a feeling that arrives beforehand. Waiting for confidence to come first is waiting for something that only comes after.</p>
    <p class="reveal">The order is backwards in almost everyone's head. We think confidence causes action, so we wait to feel confident. But it runs the other way: action causes confidence. <strong>You act your way into feeling ready, never the reverse.</strong> The woman who started before she felt ready is the only one who ever feels ready — because she has proof now, and proof is where confidence actually comes from.</p>

    <div class="marginal reveal">
      <p>The feeling you're waiting for is on the other side of the thing you're avoiding. <em>Start badly, start small, but start — readiness is downstream of the first ugly attempt.</em></p>
      <span class="note">confidence is a result, not a prerequisite ✶</span>
    </div>

    <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">04</span><div class="num">Chapter Four</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="decide">Deciding who you are first</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">The most powerful mindset move is to decide who you are before you feel like it's true — to adopt the identity of the person who does this, and let your actions follow the decision rather than wait for permission.</p>
    <p class="reveal">This isn't affirmations. It's a genuine decision about self-concept, made in advance of the evidence. You decide "I'm someone who builds things and finishes them," and then you do the small next thing not to become that person, but because you've decided you already are. <strong>The identity comes first; the behaviour confirms it.</strong></p>

    <div class="box check reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✓</span> You've made the shift when</span><ul>
      <li>You act without waiting to feel like it</li>
      <li>You make decisions instead of collecting more information</li>
      <li>You finish things because that's who you are now</li>
      <li>You stop asking permission you were never required to have</li>
    </ul></div>

    <div class="chapter reveal"><span class="bignum">05</span><div class="num">Chapter Five</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="start">Naming it in public is the intervention</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">The most effective way to break a months-long stall is to name the thing you've been avoiding, out loud, to other people. Saying it in public turns a private intention into an accountable one — and that's most of the intervention.</p>
    <p class="reveal">The thing you've been "about to start" for three months stays stuck partly because it lives only in your head, where it costs nothing to keep postponing. <strong>Said out loud to people who'll remember, it becomes real — and real things get done.</strong> This is why doing the mindset work alone is so much harder than doing it in a room with others facing the same wall.</p>

